{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, when many evils and troubles find him, then this song shall answer before him as a witness.</b> This is the song of Ha'azinu that follows immediately, for there it speaks of this beginning of exile — those driven away and scattered." "<b>For it shall not be forgotten from the mouth of his seed, for I know his inclination which he is doing today.</b> He says: from what he is doing today I know his inclination, that he is destined to engage in idolatry (עבודת זרה); and therefore, when the troubles find him, this song written in the Torah will testify against him, for the Torah shall not be forgotten from the mouth of his seed, and the witnesses shall endure forever. And he said 'for I know his inclination,' in the language of Scripture (Psalms 103:14) 'כי הוא ידע יצרנו', and they said in the Midrash: 'כי הוא ידע יצרנו' — woe to the dough, for the baker himself testifies against it that it is bad!'" "<b>And it appears to me</b> from the language of Scripture that it alludes that a time will come when the Torah shall be forgotten, and that is the time of the abolition of the evil inclination (יצר הרע), which is the time of the resurrection, when the Torah is destined to be abolished in that time, except for Purim; and this is what it says: 'for it shall not be forgotten from the mouth of his seed, for I know his inclination' — that is, as long as 'I know his inclination'; but when his inclination is abolished, it shall be forgotten. And likewise our Rabbis of blessed memory said (רז״ל): the Torah is destined to be forgotten from Israel, and this shall be in the time of the resurrection and not in the days of the Messiah, God forbid (ח״ו), for behold our Rabbis of blessed memory said: there is no difference between this world and the days of the Messiah except subjugation to the kingdoms alone; but the endurance of the Torah is for us and for our children forever, as long as this physical world continues according to its present order. But at the resurrection of the dead, it is destined to change for the better and to operate according to a different order, and there they said that the Torah shall be forgotten from Israel." "<b>And likewise</b> they said further explicitly in the Midrash on Proverbs (Proverbs 9:2) 'טבחה טבחה מסכה יינה אף ערכה שלחנה' — 'She has slaughtered her slaughter, mixed her wine, and also set her table': this is Esther, who set a time for Haman and made a banquet for him; 'אף ערכה שלחנה' — she set for herself a table in this world and in the world to come, for all the appointed times shall be abolished, but the days of Purim shall not be abolished, as it is said (Esther 9:28) 'וימי הפורים האלה לא יעברו מתוך היהודים וזכרם לא יסוף מזרעם'."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:53.372249304Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}